`rustdoc` enables `#[doc(auto_cfg)]` at the crate level by default. It automatically displays `#[cfg(...)]` compatibility markers.

To hide specific predicates, use `hide`:

```rust
#![doc(auto_cfg(
    hide(unix),
    hide(feature, values("default")),
))]
```

Examples:

```rust
// Hide the `unix` cfg everywhere in the crate.
#![doc(auto_cfg(hide(unix)))]
```

```rust
// Hide only these specific values.
#![doc(auto_cfg(
    hide(feature, values("something"))),
    hide(target_os, values("linux")),
))]
```

To hide a key with all its values:

```rust
#![doc(auto_cfg(
    hide(feature, values(any())),
))]
```

To hide a predicate only when it has no value:

```rust
#![doc(auto_cfg(
    hide(feature, values("something", none())),
))]
```

Rustdoc itself hides `doc` and `doctest` attributes by default. `auto_cfg(hide(...))` is for suppressing configuration predicates from the generated visual markers, and applies when `auto_cfg` is enabled.
